Option A (preferred, eliminates the foot-gun for all future callers): switch DiGraph to petgraph::stable_graph::StableDiGraph. StableDiGraph does not relocate nodes on removal; NodeIndex values remain valid until that node is explicitly removed, so node_map never becomes stale.

Option B (targeted fix without changing the graph type): after calling self.graph.remove_node(idx), check if a displaced node now occupies idx and update node_map accordingly:

```rust
pub fn remove_node(&mut self, fact_id: i64) {
    let Some(idx) = self.node_map.remove(&fact_id) else {
        return;
    };
    self.graph.remove_node(idx);
    // petgraph swap-remove: the node that was last (now at `idx`) needs
    // its node_map entry updated. self.graph[idx] holds its fact_id.
    if let Some(&displaced_fact_id) = self.graph.node_weight(idx) {
        self.node_map.insert(displaced_fact_id, idx);
    }
}
```

Note: node_weight returns None when idx is out-of-range (the removed node was the last one, no displacement), so the guard is safe.

