Fix the implementation so node_map is kept consistent after each swap_remove. After `self.graph.remove_node(idx)`, if petgraph placed a node at `idx` (i.e., `idx` was not the last slot), read back its weight (the fact_id) and update node_map:

```rust
pub fn remove_node(&mut self, fact_id: i64) {
    let Some(idx) = self.node_map.remove(&fact_id) else {
        return;
    };
    self.graph.remove_node(idx);
    // petgraph::DiGraph uses swap_remove: the former last node is now at `idx`.
    // Update node_map so that node's entry points to its new index.
    if let Some(&relocated_fact_id) = self.graph.node_weight(idx) {
        self.node_map.insert(relocated_fact_id, idx);
    }
}
```

Alternatively, switch `DiGraph` to `petgraph::stable_graph::StableDiGraph`, which does not invalidate NodeIndex values on removal (uses tombstones instead of swap_remove). That eliminates the invariant entirely at the cost of slightly higher memory use.

